 "title": "Top K Frequent Elements",
 "slug": "top-k-frequent-elements",
 "category": "arrays-hashing",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/top-k-frequent-elements/",
 "pattern": "Count, then keep the top k in a min-heap",
 "statement": "Given an integer array `nums` and an integer `k`, return the `k` values that appear **most often**. The answer may be in any order.",
 "example": {
  "input": "nums = [5,3,3,5,5,8], k = 2",
  "output": "[3,5]",
  "why": "5 appears three times and 3 appears twice; 8 appears only once."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "nums = [1], k = 1",
   "output": "[1]"
  },
  {
   "input": "nums = [1,1,1,2,2,3], k = 2",
   "output": "[1,2]"
  },
  {
   "input": "nums = [4,4,6,6,6,7], k = 1",
   "output": "[6]"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "First count how often every number appears, with a hash map.",
  "We then need the k largest counts. Sorting all of them works, but we never need the full order — only the top k.",
  "Keep a **min-heap of size k**. Its top is the *weakest* candidate. Push each (count, number); whenever the heap grows past k, pop the top. What survives is the top k."
 ],
 "code": "import heapq\n\nclass Solution:\n    def topKFrequent(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> List[int]:\n        frequency_map = {}\n        for num in nums:\n            frequency_map[num] = frequency_map.get(num, 0) + 1\n\n        min_heap = []\n        for num, frequency in frequency_map.items():\n            heapq.heappush(min_heap, (frequency, num))\n            if len(min_heap) > k:\n                heapq.heappop(min_heap)\n\n        return [num for frequency, num in min_heap]",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n log k)",
  "timeWhy": "Counting is O(n). Each distinct number is pushed into a heap that never holds more than k + 1 items.",
  "space": "O(n)",
  "spaceWhy": "The count map can hold every distinct number; the heap holds at most k + 1."
 },
